WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:05.160
It's been a banner year for horror, especially
with the success of Jordan Peele's Get Out.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.780
But for every movie that gets its time in
the spotlight, there are many that fly right

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:09.830
under the radar.

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:13.669
These are some of the most underrated horror
movies of the last 15 years.

00:00:13.669 --> 00:00:15.179
The Descent

00:00:15.179 --> 00:00:19.010
This all-female flick is a favorite among
horror buffs, but for some reason it rarely

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:20.590
gets mentioned in most roundups.

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:25.080
The 2005 film follows Sarah on her way to
a much-needed girl's trip to go exploring

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:28.230
in a cave after the tragic loss of both her
daughter and husband.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.160
Sounds like a great idea, right?

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.439
Unfortunately, the girls aren't alone in this
endless cave.

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:36.910
Add in the extra drama of the frenemy relationships
between the women, and you get a masterpiece

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:39.870
of tension, claustrophobia, and gross-out
horror.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:41.860
The House of the Devil

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:46.190
It's a classic story: it's the 1980s, and
college student Samantha accepts a babysitting

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:48.660
job where things just get stranger and stranger.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:53.039
A 2009 horror release from director Ti West,
The House of the Devil pays homage to many

00:00:53.039 --> 00:00:57.310
classic horror tropes: demonic possession,
the nervous babysitter, and a major twist

00:00:57.310 --> 00:00:59.609
that happens surprisingly early in the film.

00:00:59.609 --> 00:01:03.469
Despite its limited release, horror fans found
their way to The House of the Devil, and critics

00:01:03.469 --> 00:01:05.220
praised its cultural awareness.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.450
Unfortunately, it never found a widespread
audience.

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:10.020
A Tale of Two Sisters

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:13.580
A dark version of an old Korean folktale that
bears a strong resemblance to Cinderella,

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:18.600
2003's A Tale of Two Sisters combines fairy
tales with all the wonderfully horrific elements

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:23.230
of K-horror, and stands above a slew of other
fantastic Korean films from the last decade.

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:27.579
Of course, an American remake was released
in 2009 under the title The Uninvited.

00:01:27.579 --> 00:01:30.119
Do yourself a favor and go straight to the
original.

00:01:30.119 --> 00:01:31.450
Thirst

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:36.130
Another fantastic Korean horror film takes
the form of 2009's Thirst, from Oldboy director

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:37.130
Park Chan-wook.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:40.760
With this story of a priest turned blood-sucking
vampire, Park turns the undead fable into

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:43.610
a delirious romance while still offering up
plenty of scares.

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:48.330
Though the film won the Jury Prize at Cannes
in 2009 and debuted at No. 1 at the Korean

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:51.340
box office, for American audiences, the movie
was easy to miss.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:55.509
If you haven't seen Thirst—or Park Chan-wook's
other work—give it a shot.

00:01:55.509 --> 00:01:58.040
You might be grossed out, but you won't be
disappointed.

00:01:58.289 --> 00:01:59.500
Pontypool

00:01:59.509 --> 00:02:02.430
After a short theatrical run in New York,
this Canadian horror movie went straight to

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:04.030
DVD in 2010.

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:08.400
Based on the novel Pontypool Changes Everything,
Pontypool tells the story of a radio DJ who's

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.580
determined to broadcast in the midst of increasing
reports of strange behavior and violent incidents

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:13.630
in a small town.

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:17.790
A fascinating psychological thriller about
the nature of violence inspired by Orson Welles'

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:22.710
radio broadcast War of the Worlds, Pontypool
is definitely a forgotten horror gem.

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:24.050
The Loved Ones

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:28.360
Usually it's a male psycho or stalker who
creates all the problems in your typical abduction-type

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:29.360
horror movie.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.910
But in this 2009 Australian horror movie,
it's a girl named Lola, and all she wants

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:34.110
is a date to the prom.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:37.740
With help from her dear old dad, Lola abducts
the object of her affection and proceeds to

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:40.570
torture the living daylights out of him at
their kitchen table.

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:43.430
Once it gets going, The Loved Ones never lets
up.

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:44.810
High Tension

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:48.180
This excellent French slasher film turns the
genre on its head.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.640
Fans of blood spatter will get more than they
could have dreamed of, and everyone who watches

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:55.600
horror movies for their cultural commentary
will have plenty to discuss after the movie.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:00.350
As part of cinema's New French Extremity movement—basically
the French version of body horror—High Tension

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:04.550
is as shockingly scary as it is psychologically
disturbing, making it a horror film for the

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:05.590
ages.

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:06.840
Green Room

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:10.710
The horrifying tale of a punk band that finds
itself trapped backstage at their concert

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:14.180
venue, Green Room was the toast of nearly
every critic's list in 2016.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:17.510
But outside of horror circles, the movie seemed
destined to remain in obscurity.

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:21.550
Luckily, film distributor A24 stepped in,
allowing the film to get a slightly wider

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:22.550
release.

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:25.950
With a fantastic cast that includes Patrick
Stewart and Anton Yelchin, Green Room is a

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:28.290
modern classic worth checking out.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:29.290
We Are What We Are

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:34.750
This 2013 American remake of a 2010 Mexican
film takes on a profoundly disturbing topic:

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:35.750
cannibalism.

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:37.630
And with this story, it's all in the family.

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:41.700
With a subject as tough as this one, both
critics and audience members alike were perplexed,

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:45.500
with The New York Times calling it "a dreamy
commentary on the ravages of extreme religious

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:46.500
observance."

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:50.580
Despite its lukewarm reception, We Are What
We Are is a well-executed, stylized addition

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:52.850
to the modern horror canon.

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:53.850
V/H/S

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:57.840
Perhaps because V/H/S is an anthology of short
films rather than one narrative film, it never

00:03:57.840 --> 00:03:59.610
got much credit as a horror movie.

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:02.700
Most of the stories are unnerving, and several
are downright terrifying.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:05.741
Critics called the collection "uneven," and
the two sequels weren't quite as good as the

00:04:05.741 --> 00:04:10.040
original, but V/H/S is definitely a unique
modern twist on horror lore.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:11.630
Inside

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:15.620
Critics praised this French horror movie on
its 2007 release, but mainstream audiences

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:18.459
outside of its native France never quite caught
the hype train.

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:22.350
Due to its popularity among dedicated horror
fans, an English-language version was made

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:26.720
by Spanish director Miguel Vivas and will
be widely released in Spain in 2017.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:28.640
But why bother with a tepid remake?

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:31.830
The original version is perfectly terrifying
as it is.

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:33.120
Evil Dead

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:37.300
This soft reboot of horror classic The Evil
Dead is beloved by die-hard horror fans who

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:38.730
know a great thing when they see it.

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:42.450
It's both a natural evolution of the series
and a completely standalone film...with plenty

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:44.160
of nods to the original, of course.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:48.270
Possibly even gorier and more horrifying than
the original, Evil Dead can't quite claim

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:49.870
mainstream appreciation.

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:53.160
But that's just fine with critics and horror
fans, who have been singing its praises for

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:54.160
years.

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:55.160
Thanks for watching!

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:56.950
